Q: Chakra-UI Text component&#160;noOfLines doesn&#39;t display right in Safari A: <pre><code>...

&lt;Text lineClamp={2} &gt;
        &lt;ReactMarkdown&gt;{collection.description}&lt;/ReactMarkdown&gt;
&lt;/Text&gt;

...
</code></pre>

Q: GDB typecasting failure in c A: <p><strong>Perhaps not meeting alignment nor padding needs?</strong></p>
<p>Code does one allocation for three different types of objects.  OP's code risks not meeting the alignment needs of the next 2 objects nor performing a correct size calculation with potential needed padding.</p>
<p>Avoid typecasting and trying to do pointer arithmetic with <code>char*</code>.  Let the compiler calculate the offsets and size.</p>
<p>Consider instead allocation for 1 <code>struct</code> of all 3 object types:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>typedef struct {
  RTTI_INT_metadata i;
  RTTI_FLOAT_metadata f;
  RTTI_STRING_metadata s;
} all3;

void init_builtin_metadataS(void){
  all3 *ptr3 = malloc(sizeof ptr3[0]);
  assert(ptr3); // Even better if (ptr3 == NULL) ... to always check allocation.

  ptr_builtin_metadata_int = &amp;ptr3-&gt;i;
  ...

  ptr_builtin_metadata_float = &amp;ptr3-&gt;f;
  ...

  ptr_builtin_metadata_string = &amp;ptr3-&gt;s;
  ...
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>We could avoid the <code>typedef</code> and use the following to allocate.  This cuts down on namespace pollution, but a tad trickier to follow.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>  struct {
    RTTI_INT_metadata i;
    RTTI_FLOAT_metadata f;
    RTTI_STRING_metadata s;
  } *ptr3 = malloc(sizeof ptr3[0]);
</code></pre>
